---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is 100.64.x.x a public IP?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: carrier-grade NAT space. If that is your outbound address, you are behind CGNAT and cannot host inbound services."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I use private IPs in my VPC?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: cloud VPCs are built on RFC 1918 ranges with public gateways handling translation."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What should I do if my device has 169.254.x.x?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "DHCP failed: restart the router/DHCP server. The address cannot reach the internet."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Are multicast ranges dangerous?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Not dangerous, but often filtered: some networks block multicast to prevent flooding."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How do I check if an IP is private?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Match it against the ranges in this table, or run it through the IP lookup: private ranges report as reserved."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Reserved and Private IP Ranges (RFC 1918 and More) | PromptDNS"
  description="Every reserved IP range: private (RFC 1918), loopback, link-local, and documentation ranges, with what each is for."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ip-and-networking/" class="hover:text-accent">IP & Networking</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Reserved IP Ranges</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Reserved IP Ranges</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Not every IP address routes on the internet. Several ranges are reserved for private networks, testing, and documentation: knowing them on sight prevents a whole family of configuration mistakes.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The complete table</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60"><table class="pdns-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">10.0.0.0/8</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Private (RFC 1918)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Enterprise networks, cloud VPCs</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">172.16.0.0/12</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Private (RFC 1918)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Mid-size networks</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">192.168.0.0/16</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Private (RFC 1918)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Home and small office</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">127.0.0.0/8</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Loopback</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The machine itself (localhost)</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">169.254.0.0/16</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Link-local</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Self-assigned when DHCP fails</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">100.64.0.0/10</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">CGNAT</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Carrier-grade NAT space</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">192.0.2.0/24 + others</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Documentation</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Examples and tutorials (TEST-NET)</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">224.0.0.0/4</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Multicast</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">One-to-many delivery</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The rules that follow</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed">Private and loopback addresses never appear in public DNS: an A record pointing at one resolves only inside your network.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Documentation ranges (192.0.2.x, 198.51.100.x, 203.0.113.x) are guaranteed safe for examples, which is why our guides use them.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Seeing 169.254.x.x on a device means DHCP failed: it self-assigned and has no real network.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is 100.64.x.x a public IP?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: carrier-grade NAT space. If that is your outbound address, you are behind CGNAT and cannot host inbound services.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I use private IPs in my VPC?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: cloud VPCs are built on RFC 1918 ranges with public gateways handling translation.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What should I do if my device has 169.254.x.x?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DHCP failed: restart the router/DHCP server. The address cannot reach the internet.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Are multicast ranges dangerous?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Not dangerous, but often filtered: some networks block multicast to prevent flooding.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I check if an IP is private?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Match it against the ranges in this table, or run it through the IP lookup: private ranges report as reserved.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/public-vs-private-ip/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Public vs private IP →</a></li><li><a href="/cidr-calculator/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">CIDR calculator →</a></li><li><a href="/ip-lookup/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">IP lookup →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
